```java
// 1. ENCAPSULATION — hide data, control access
class Account {
    private double balance;             // hidden
    public void deposit(double amt) { if(amt>0) balance+=amt; } // controlled
}

// 2. INHERITANCE — reuse and extend
class SavingsAccount extends Account { // IS-A Account
    double interestRate;
    void applyInterest() { /* extends Account */ }
}

// 3. POLYMORPHISM — one interface, many behaviors
Account acc = new SavingsAccount(); // parent ref, child object
acc.deposit(100);                   // runtime dispatch — SavingsAccount version if overridden

// 4. ABSTRACTION — hide implementation, show interface
interface Payment {
    void process(double amount); // WHAT, not HOW
}
class CreditCardPayment implements Payment {
    public void process(double amount) { /* HOW hidden inside */ }
}
```

These four principles work together: **abstraction** defines the contract, **encapsulation** protects the implementation, **inheritance** allows reuse, and **polymorphism** makes code work flexibly with many types.

```java
// OVERLOADING — same class, different parameter lists
class Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
    double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }     // overloaded
    int add(int a, int b, int c) { return a + b + c; }   // overloaded again
}
// Compiler chooses at compile time based on argument types

// OVERRIDING — subclass, same signature
class Animal {
    void speak() { System.out.println("..."); }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void speak() { System.out.println("Woof!"); }  // overrides
}
Animal a = new Dog();
a.speak(); // Woof! — JVM decides at runtime
```

```java
// Abstract class — can have state, constructor, protected methods
abstract class DatabaseDAO {
    protected Connection conn;           // STATE
    protected DatabaseDAO(String url) {  // CONSTRUCTOR
        this.conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url);
    }
    abstract List<Object> findAll();     // abstract — subclass implements
    protected void closeConn() { /* concrete + protected */ }
}

// Interface — no state, no constructor, public only
interface Repository {
    List<Object> findAll();              // abstract (implicit)
    default void printAll() {           // default since Java 8
        findAll().forEach(System.out::println);
    }
    // No state, no protected, no constructor
}
```

```java
// INHERITANCE (IS-A) — tight coupling, fragile
class Stack extends Vector {  // Java's mistake!
    // Stack IS-NOT really a Vector — push/pop via addElement() leaks!
    // External code can call vector.add() and bypass stack semantics
}

// COMPOSITION (HAS-A) — flexible, loosely coupled
class Stack<T> {
    private List<T> storage;       // HAS-A list (swappable!)

    Stack() { this.storage = new ArrayList<>(); }
    Stack(List<T> storage) { this.storage = storage; } // inject impl

    void push(T item) { storage.add(item); }
    T pop() { return storage.remove(storage.size() - 1); }
}
// Now the Stack controls its interface, internal impl is hidden
```

Java's own `java.util.Stack extends Vector` is widely cited as a design mistake — the IS-A relationship was wrong, and it exposed Vector methods that break stack semantics. The composition version is better: `Stack` HAS-A `List`, and you can inject `ArrayList` or `LinkedList` without changing `Stack`.

Here's a notification system that uses all four principles:

```java
// ABSTRACTION — interface defines the contract
interface NotificationService {
    void send(String recipient, String message);
}

// ABSTRACTION + ENCAPSULATION — abstract class with hidden state
abstract class BaseNotification implements NotificationService {
    private final String apiKey;       // ENCAPSULATION — hidden
    private int sentCount = 0;         // ENCAPSULATION — tracked internally

    BaseNotification(String apiKey) { this.apiKey = apiKey; }

    @Override
    public final void send(String recipient, String message) { // Template Method
        validate(recipient);           // common logic
        doSend(recipient, message);    // POLYMORPHISM — subclass implements
        sentCount++;                   // common post-send tracking
    }
    protected abstract void doSend(String recipient, String message);
    protected String getApiKey() { return apiKey; }
}

// INHERITANCE — EmailService IS-A BaseNotification
class EmailService extends BaseNotification {
    EmailService(String key) { super(key); }
    protected void doSend(String to, String msg) {
        System.out.println("Email to " + to + ": " + msg);
    }
}

class SMSService extends BaseNotification {
    SMSService(String key) { super(key); }
    protected void doSend(String to, String msg) {
        System.out.println("SMS to " + to + ": " + msg);
    }
}

// POLYMORPHISM — caller uses interface, doesn't know concrete type
NotificationService svc = new EmailService("key123");
svc.send("user@example.com", "Welcome!"); // runtime dispatch
```
